.TH DIKTAT 1 "2026-08-23" "diktat 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
diktat \- voice dictation that types where you are looking
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B diktat
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B diktat
transcribes speech locally and types it into whatever window has focus. A
daemon holds one speech model for the whole session, so a dictation costs the
time it takes to say it rather than the time it takes to load a model. No audio
leaves the machine, and nothing needs a network once a model has been fetched.
.PP
It also transcribes recordings into documents that say who spoke.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B daemon
Run for the session: hold the model, record while toggled on, transcribe, and
type. Started by the systemd user service; it never records by itself and never
exits by itself. With no model downloaded it waits, and loads the first one
.B diktat model
names.
.TP
.B toggle
Start or stop recording. Bind this to a key.
.TP
.B repeat
Type the last transcription again.
.TP
.BR model " [" \fImodel\fR ]
List the speech models, or switch to one, downloading it first if the cache
lacks it. A model may be named or given by its number in the listing.
.TP
.BR transcribe " [" \fIoptions\fR "] " \fIrecording\fR
Transcribe a recording into a markdown document with speaker labels. Any format
.BR ffmpeg (1)
reads is accepted. Given a
.I .json
turns file from an earlier run, the document is rendered again without
transcribing anything.
.TP
.BR tx\-model " [" \fIpipeline\fR ]
List the pipelines that produce speaker-labelled transcripts, or choose one.
The column to read is Speakers: everything descended from one diarizer stops at
four.
.TP
.B version
Report the release, the source it was built from where that is not the release
itself, and whether the running daemon is the same build.
.SH TRANSCRIBE OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-o " path"
Write the transcript here. The default is beside the audio.
.TP
.B \-timestamps
Put the time of each turn in the document. Off by default: a timestamp is worth
having when the document is an index into the audio and in the way when it is
something to read.
.TP
.BI \-model " pipeline"
Use this pipeline instead of the one
.B tx\-model
chose.
.TP
.BI \-diarizer " gguf"
Attribute speakers with this model instead of the pipeline's. For a diarizer
that is not in the menu yet.
.TP
.BI \-lang " language"
The language of the recording.
.B \-lang list
prints what each pipeline takes.
.TP
.BI \-s " n" ", " \-speakers " n"
How many people are in the recording, when that is known. Only the entries
with a clustering diarizer take it; the rest are built around a fixed number
of speakers and refuse it rather than ignoring it. Worth giving: the estimate
cannot tell one person recorded two ways from two people, and on three voices
it has split one of them in two.
.TP
.BI \-chunk " duration"
The longest piece to transcribe at once. The default is what the pipeline can
hold.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/diktat/config.toml
Optional. Sets the model to start on, per-application paste methods, and
whether to keep a history file. An unknown key is an error rather than being
ignored.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/diktat/
The remembered model choice, and
.IR status ,
which is Pango markup for a status bar: REC while recording, TX while
transcribing, LOAD while a model is being read, and ERR when the last press
put no words on screen \(em no model, a microphone that delivered nothing, or
text that could not be typed, which
.B diktat repeat
still has. The journal says which.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/diktat/
The session's files: the daemon's PID, the model in use, the last
transcription, and what the daemon is doing. Emptied at logout.
.TP
.I ~/.cache/diktat/models/
Downloaded models. Nothing is bundled and nothing is fetched at install time.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B DIKTAT_GPU
.B 0
forces the CPU;
.B 1
takes whatever GPU the library picks. Unset, a discrete GPU is used when there
is one and the CPU otherwise, because an integrated GPU shares memory bandwidth
with the CPU it would be replacing.
.TP
.B DIKTAT_DEBUG
Log how things happened as well as what happened: audio levels, per-stage
timings, and where a model load's time went.
.SH TYPING
Typing has no portable tool. On wlroots compositors \(em sway, Hyprland, river,
wayfire, labwc, niri \(em
.BR wtype (1)
works. GNOME and KDE do not implement the protocol it needs, so install
.BR ydotool (1)
and run
.BR ydotoold (8)
there. On X11, install
.BR xdotool (1).
diktat tries them in order and names the ones that are missing when it cannot
type.
.PP
The paste methods borrow the clipboard, which follows the session in the same
way:
.BR wl\-copy (1)
and
.BR wl\-paste (1)
on Wayland,
.BR xclip (1)
or
.BR xsel (1)
on X11.
.SH SESSIONS
The daemon is a user service and inherits nothing of the graphical session, so
it finds one when it has something to type: the compositor's own process
carries
.B WAYLAND_DISPLAY
or
.BR DISPLAY ,
and on X11 the
.B XAUTHORITY
that goes with it. Wayland is looked for first, because a Wayland session runs
Xwayland and so holds both.
.PP
Two live sessions is an error rather than a choice: either is a plausible
target and the wrong one puts the dictation on the other screen.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ffmpeg (1),
.BR wtype (1),
.BR ydotool (1),
.BR xdotool (1),
.BR xclip (1),
.BR systemctl (1)
.PP
The project documentation covers the model menu, the speaker-labelling
pipeline and what it is measured at:
.PP
https://github.com/christian\-oudard/diktat
